WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.789 --> 00:00:05.740
Here is something I want to address that has
come up quite a bit because of what we're

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:11.630
experiencing right now since the killing of
George Floyd by Derek Schavan.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:17.630
There are people on the left and on the right
who will sometimes try to tout their nonracist,

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:22.890
bona fide days by using some version of the
phrase, I don't see color.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:26.800
Now, different people will use that phrase
to mean different things.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.930
There are people who will sometimes use the
phrase, I don't see color when they're called

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:34.990
out about some policy that will, like, disproportionately
affect minorities.

00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:36.620
They'll say, I don't see color.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:39.520
I'm just talking about policy.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.830
Sometimes you will see people say, I don't
see color in the sense that they say they

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:48.490
are color blind in their political views.

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:54.600
This is a very dumb phrase, and I would suggest
that it not be used anymore.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:58.920
And I want to be clear that I understand what
people often mean when they say, I don't see

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.329
color and I'm going to give you a better way
to talk about it, that that's more credible

00:01:02.329 --> 00:01:06.640
because I don't see color doesn't really make
any sense, because all of the science tells

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:08.320
us we very much see color.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:09.320
We just do.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:10.320
Doesn't mean we're racist.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:11.580
That doesn't mean we discriminate.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:18.750
But the instant that we meet people, whether
it's Western individuals in developed countries

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:25.630
or remote tribes in the Amazon or the Australian
outback, we almost instantly are assessing

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:30.760
and assimilating what we believe to be the
gender of the person we are talking to, the

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:33.600
age of the person we are talking to.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.220
And the race of the person we are talking
to.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:40.150
To the extent that you can discern that from
looking at them.

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:46.500
The instant I walk up to a cashier at my grocery
store, I am instantly making an assessment

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:52.060
of I'm speaking to a roughly 30 year old Hispanic
female, for example.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:58.420
We all see color and we all are instantly
assessing it because it's just part of being

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.620
human and living in a society.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.780
Is there any situation in which someone could
come up to you and say, hey, remember the

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:07.040
gardener that came to the house last week?

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:08.959
Do you remember if they were male or female?

00:02:08.959 --> 00:02:10.899
No, I have no recollection of it.

00:02:10.899 --> 00:02:11.959
Of course you do.

00:02:11.959 --> 00:02:13.049
Of course you do.

00:02:13.049 --> 00:02:16.340
Was the nurse that did your Kovik test.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:21.299
Was it like a 20 something nurse or was it
like a 60 something year old nurse?

00:02:21.299 --> 00:02:23.150
I have no I don't remember.

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:27.779
Was your bank teller yesterday white, black,
Asian or Hispanic?

00:02:27.779 --> 00:02:29.180
I have no idea.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:33.459
We all know it unless, you know, barring some
kind of memory or cognitive issue.

00:02:33.459 --> 00:02:38.269
So when I hear people say things like I don't
see color in my interactions with the world,

00:02:38.269 --> 00:02:39.269
people are just people.

00:02:39.269 --> 00:02:40.269
Give me a break.

00:02:40.269 --> 00:02:41.269
You do.

00:02:41.269 --> 00:02:42.500
You see gender and you see age.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:46.309
And we are immediately assessing race and
color as well.

00:02:46.309 --> 00:02:47.520
We all see each other.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.519
We recognize how someone might look similar
or different from us.

00:02:51.519 --> 00:02:53.230
And that is OK.

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:58.349
What might actually be accurate to say and
where I think the focus should be is I don't

00:02:58.349 --> 00:03:04.069
judge people on the basis of their appearance,
which includes race.

00:03:04.069 --> 00:03:05.069
That might be accurate.

00:03:05.069 --> 00:03:10.079
Now, a lot of us still have implicit and preconceived
notions about people on the basis of these

00:03:10.079 --> 00:03:11.079
characteristics.

00:03:11.079 --> 00:03:13.959
In many cases, they're relatively benign,
and that's fine.

00:03:13.959 --> 00:03:15.799
Sometimes they are discriminatory.

00:03:15.799 --> 00:03:20.140
But I actually want to go even further imagined
that it were true.

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:21.830
We don't notice people's race.

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:23.849
We really don't see color.

00:03:23.849 --> 00:03:24.950
When.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:26.049
If.

00:03:26.049 --> 00:03:27.370
Were to be the case.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:33.109
It would immediately dismiss the reality that
there are racial injustices even today in

00:03:33.109 --> 00:03:34.109
society.

00:03:34.109 --> 00:03:35.659
If I didn't see color.

00:03:35.659 --> 00:03:39.379
If someone said to me, hey, can you snap your
fingers?

00:03:39.379 --> 00:03:44.079
And you could you could snap your fingers
and become black right now and society, would

00:03:44.079 --> 00:03:45.249
you want to do it?

00:03:45.249 --> 00:03:47.989
If I didn't see color, I'd have no way to
answer that question.

00:03:47.989 --> 00:03:49.249
Now, of course, I see color.

00:03:49.249 --> 00:03:55.739
And of course, I recognize that I would likely
be worse off in society if I were black than

00:03:55.739 --> 00:03:58.930
being white, Hispanic as I am and passing
as white.

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:03.840
I see color and that allows me to recognize
that if someone says you could snap a finger

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.599
and be black, do you want to do it?

00:04:05.599 --> 00:04:08.129
No, I'd be worse off in society if I did that.

00:04:08.129 --> 00:04:12.169
I recognized that in part because I see color.

00:04:12.169 --> 00:04:19.730
We also all see color, by the way, by virtue
of seeing how different people are treated

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:22.120
in society by different institutions.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:25.860
And we're, of course, seeing that very much
with the George Floyd situation.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:28.550
So I think that that just about covers it.

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:33.930
I find the phrase I don't see color to be
to be pretty silly, quite frankly.

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:37.360
And for people who are trying to make the
case that they're not making judgments about

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:40.340
others on the basis of race that I like.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:41.340
It may be true.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:42.340
It may not be true.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:44.110
It's certainly something to aspire to.

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:45.300
But let's be more accurate when we talk about
it.

